1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,720
This is a QR code.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:04,200
It holds one short line of text.

3
00:00:04,919 --> 00:00:06,919
The phone reads it straight away.

4
00:00:07,703 --> 00:00:09,923
Now let's tear a piece out of it.

5
00:00:10,230 --> 00:00:13,890
And scan it again.
The same text. Every letter.

6
00:00:14,617 --> 00:00:18,217
Part of that code is gone.
Not hard to read. Gone.

7
00:00:19,095 --> 00:00:22,375
So how can a phone read something
that is no longer there?

8
00:00:24,905 --> 00:00:27,105
Here's the same problem, made smaller.

9
00:00:27,205 --> 00:00:29,405
Someone sends you eight bits.

10
00:00:30,125 --> 00:00:31,825
On the way, three of them are lost.

11
00:00:32,536 --> 00:00:37,056
Each missing bit is a zero or a one,
so there are eight ways to fill the gaps.

12
00:00:37,836 --> 00:00:40,756
And nothing in what arrived
tells you which one is right.

13
00:00:41,102 --> 00:00:43,622
And yet the phone just did exactly that.

14
00:00:44,142 --> 00:00:45,342
It wasn't guessing.

15
00:00:45,902 --> 00:00:48,522
The answer was sent
before anything was lost.

16
00:00:49,266 --> 00:00:52,086
The trick is to send more
than the message itself.

17
00:00:52,882 --> 00:00:56,002
The simplest way
is to say everything three times.

18
00:00:56,722 --> 00:01:00,022
A zero is sent as three zeros,
a one as three ones.

19
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:03,580
Noise flips one of them,
and you receive one, zero, one.

20
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:06,080
Two votes against one.

21
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:08,280
It was almost certainly a one.

22
00:01:08,824 --> 00:01:12,844
If noise flips one bit in ten,
at random and independently,

23
00:01:13,224 --> 00:01:16,684
a vote of three goes wrong
less than three times in a hundred.

24
00:01:17,004 --> 00:01:19,004
But you've tripled everything you send.

25
00:01:19,704 --> 00:01:23,884
To get closer to perfect, you'd repeat
five times, then nine, then a hundred...

26
00:01:23,964 --> 00:01:26,164
and the message slows to a crawl.

27
00:01:27,030 --> 00:01:29,290
For a long time,
that looked like the price.

28
00:01:30,050 --> 00:01:33,330
Fewer errors, slower messages.
Keep that in mind.

29
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:41,000
In the late 1940s, Richard Hamming
at Bell Labs had a problem of his own.

30
00:01:41,505 --> 00:01:43,245
He mostly got time on a relay computer

31
00:01:43,475 --> 00:01:46,945
at weekends,
when nobody was there to watch it.

32
00:01:47,425 --> 00:01:49,765
The machine could tell
when it had made a mistake.

33
00:01:50,465 --> 00:01:52,765
It would try the step again,
three times...

34
00:01:52,845 --> 00:01:54,405
and then drop his job.

35
00:01:54,725 --> 00:01:57,545
Two weekends in a row, he lost his work.

36
00:01:58,171 --> 00:02:01,251
His question, in his own words:
"If the machine can locate

37
00:02:01,481 --> 00:02:04,591
there is an error,
why can it not locate where it is?"

38
00:02:04,811 --> 00:02:08,471
The actual language, he admitted,
was probably a bit stronger.

39
00:02:11,011 --> 00:02:14,671
His answer takes four bits of message
and adds three check bits.

40
00:02:15,071 --> 00:02:16,851
Seven bits in all.

41
00:02:17,466 --> 00:02:20,266
Each check bit looks after
a different group of positions,

42
00:02:20,626 --> 00:02:23,586
and makes sure that group
holds an even number of ones.

43
00:02:24,239 --> 00:02:26,679
The groups overlap in a careful pattern.

44
00:02:27,439 --> 00:02:30,759
Every position belongs
to its own combination of groups.

45
00:02:31,505 --> 00:02:33,005
Now flip one bit.

46
00:02:33,385 --> 00:02:35,225
Say, the one in position six.

47
00:02:35,613 --> 00:02:37,953
The receiver runs the three checks again.

48
00:02:38,273 --> 00:02:42,953
The first one passes.
The second fails. The third one fails.

49
00:02:43,607 --> 00:02:46,027
Write a failed check as one,
a passed check as zero,

50
00:02:46,257 --> 00:02:48,447
and read them from the last to the first.

51
00:02:49,287 --> 00:02:50,367
One, one, zero.

52
00:02:50,447 --> 00:02:52,647
In binary, that's six.

53
00:02:53,392 --> 00:02:56,092
The checks don't just say
that something went wrong.

54
00:02:56,612 --> 00:02:58,472
They point at the exact bit.

55
00:02:59,165 --> 00:03:01,765
Flip it back,
and the message is whole again.

56
00:03:02,385 --> 00:03:04,505
Nobody had to send anything twice.

57
00:03:05,279 --> 00:03:06,759
So that's the answer.

58
00:03:07,439 --> 00:03:10,619
The receiver doesn't rebuild
lost information out of nothing.

59
00:03:11,279 --> 00:03:13,499
The sender packed it in, ahead of time.

60
00:03:16,039 --> 00:03:17,959
Not every check can do that.

61
00:03:18,719 --> 00:03:21,779
A checksum, like the one in our video
about Btrfs,

62
00:03:21,859 --> 00:03:26,039
can tell you a block is damaged,
but not what the original data were.

63
00:03:26,454 --> 00:03:28,194
And there's a second kind of damage.

64
00:03:28,274 --> 00:03:30,614
Sometimes a bit isn't wrong.

65
00:03:30,694 --> 00:03:34,674
It's just missing,
and you know exactly where the gap is.

66
00:03:35,301 --> 00:03:38,361
That's called an erasure,
and erasures are easier.

67
00:03:39,087 --> 00:03:41,267
Send four numbers,
four, seven, two and nine,

68
00:03:41,497 --> 00:03:44,478
and a fifth one: their sum, twenty-two.

69
00:03:44,558 --> 00:03:47,098
If any one of them goes missing,
the others tell you what it was.

70
00:03:47,328 --> 00:03:50,958
Four, seven, something, nine, twenty-two.

71
00:03:51,038 --> 00:03:52,898
It has to be two.

72
00:03:53,490 --> 00:03:57,930
Servers protect their disks with a similar
kind of check, spread across the drives.

73
00:03:58,730 --> 00:04:02,250
When one drive dies,
its contents are worked out from the rest.

74
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:06,780
So how much extra do you need?

75
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:12,000
Repetition says: to get errors down to
nothing, you have to slow down to nothing.

76
00:04:12,578 --> 00:04:15,218
Two years before Hamming's paper came out,

77
00:04:15,298 --> 00:04:18,578
his colleague Claude Shannon
had published a stranger answer.

78
00:04:19,159 --> 00:04:22,579
Every channel, a radio link,
a cable, a disc, has a limit.

79
00:04:23,299 --> 00:04:24,979
A top speed for information.

80
00:04:25,871 --> 00:04:30,011
Below that limit, errors can be made
as rare as you like, without slowing down.

81
00:04:30,411 --> 00:04:34,231
Not by repeating more. By coding smarter.

82
00:04:34,995 --> 00:04:37,515
Above it, no code in the world will help.

83
00:04:38,341 --> 00:04:42,801
But there was a catch.
Shannon proved that such codes exist.

84
00:04:43,341 --> 00:04:46,441
He didn't show how to build one
that anybody could use.

85
00:04:47,039 --> 00:04:50,859
His proof said a code picked at random
would almost certainly be good.

86
00:04:51,559 --> 00:04:54,379
But the obvious way to decode it
means comparing what arrived

87
00:04:54,609 --> 00:04:56,499
with every possible message...

88
00:04:56,919 --> 00:04:58,859
and for a block of a thousand bits,

89
00:04:59,019 --> 00:05:01,579
there are more of those
than atoms in the universe.

90
00:05:02,347 --> 00:05:04,547
So Shannon's paper wasn't a solution.

91
00:05:05,167 --> 00:05:06,367
It was a promise.

92
00:05:06,947 --> 00:05:10,607
And engineers spent the next
forty-five years trying to keep it.

93
00:05:13,227 --> 00:05:15,287
Hamming's code was a first step.

94
00:05:15,847 --> 00:05:17,947
It fixes one wrong bit in seven.

95
00:05:18,295 --> 00:05:20,475
But real damage comes in bursts.

96
00:05:21,335 --> 00:05:25,235
A scratch or a crackle of static
takes out hundreds of bits in a row.

97
00:05:25,838 --> 00:05:29,218
In 1960, Irving Reed and Gustave Solomon,

98
00:05:29,298 --> 00:05:33,798
at MIT's Lincoln Laboratory,
published a paper five pages long.

99
00:05:34,382 --> 00:05:37,422
Their codes work on whole bytes,
not single bits.

100
00:05:38,242 --> 00:05:42,102
If a burst wrecks all eight bits
of a byte, that's still just one bad byte.

101
00:05:43,056 --> 00:05:45,636
One common version adds
thirty-two check bytes

102
00:05:45,866 --> 00:05:48,356
to two hundred and twenty-three
bytes of data.

103
00:05:49,061 --> 00:05:51,881
It can fix any sixteen bytes
that arrive wrong...

104
00:05:52,121 --> 00:05:55,021
or rebuild thirty-two
that never arrive at all.

105
00:05:55,579 --> 00:05:57,759
That's how a CD plays through a scratch.

106
00:05:58,719 --> 00:06:01,339
Its two Reed-Solomon codes
can fully repair a gap

107
00:06:01,569 --> 00:06:05,619
of about four thousand bits,
two and a half millimetres of track.

108
00:06:06,058 --> 00:06:08,798
And it's why the torn QR code
still worked.

109
00:06:09,298 --> 00:06:12,358
At its strongest setting,
a QR code can rebuild

110
00:06:12,588 --> 00:06:16,778
up to about thirty percent of its bytes
and still give back every letter.

111
00:06:19,638 --> 00:06:25,498
Voyager 2 left Earth in 1977
already carrying a Reed-Solomon encoder.

112
00:06:25,827 --> 00:06:29,287
But at Jupiter and Saturn,
it guarded everything but the pictures

113
00:06:29,517 --> 00:06:31,827
with an older code, by Marcel Golay,

114
00:06:31,907 --> 00:06:35,027
which sent one extra bit
for every bit of data.

115
00:06:35,758 --> 00:06:39,138
For Uranus and Neptune,
with the signal getting weaker,

116
00:06:39,218 --> 00:06:42,818
engineers switched Reed-Solomon on,
on top of a second code

117
00:06:43,048 --> 00:06:45,118
that every bit already went through.

118
00:06:45,710 --> 00:06:50,090
With that second code alone, about five
bits in a thousand were still wrong.

119
00:06:50,930 --> 00:06:53,690
With Reed-Solomon on top,
about one in a million.

120
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:57,720
Noise also comes in bursts,
so Voyager shuffled

121
00:06:57,950 --> 00:07:00,660
the bytes of four blocks together
before sending them.

122
00:07:01,144 --> 00:07:04,744
A burst that would have wrecked one block
now takes a few bytes from each...

123
00:07:04,884 --> 00:07:07,464
few enough for every block
to repair itself.

124
00:07:08,237 --> 00:07:12,637
The rest of how Neptune's pictures
got home is in our video about Voyager.

125
00:07:13,611 --> 00:07:16,271
But even this was still
short of Shannon's limit...

126
00:07:16,351 --> 00:07:19,751
and a code that could get much closer
already existed.

127
00:07:20,733 --> 00:07:26,273
In 1960, in his doctoral thesis at MIT,
Robert Gallager described codes

128
00:07:26,503 --> 00:07:30,853
made of many simple checks,
each one looking at only a few bits.

129
00:07:31,539 --> 00:07:32,759
Decoding them meant the checks

130
00:07:32,839 --> 00:07:35,839
passing guesses back and forth,
round after round...

131
00:07:35,919 --> 00:07:39,399
far too much
for the computers of the 1960s.

132
00:07:39,739 --> 00:07:43,739
So the idea was put away,
and mostly forgotten for thirty years.

133
00:07:46,069 --> 00:07:49,849
In 1993,
a team led by two French engineers,

134
00:07:49,929 --> 00:07:51,969
Claude Berrou and Alain Glavieux,

135
00:07:52,049 --> 00:07:54,949
presented turbo codes
at a conference in Geneva.

136
00:07:55,722 --> 00:07:57,702
Their results were so close
to Shannon's limit

137
00:07:57,932 --> 00:08:01,062
that many experts assumed
they'd made a mistake.

138
00:08:01,622 --> 00:08:05,042
Probably a factor of two,
somewhere in their calculations.

139
00:08:06,273 --> 00:08:09,233
Within a year,
other labs had confirmed it.

140
00:08:10,161 --> 00:08:13,941
Turbo codes also worked
by passing guesses back and forth,

141
00:08:14,061 --> 00:08:16,261
and that sent people back to Gallager.

142
00:08:16,828 --> 00:08:21,628
In 1995 and 1996,
David MacKay and Radford Neal

143
00:08:21,858 --> 00:08:25,688
rediscovered his codes,
and showed they could get just as close.

144
00:08:26,375 --> 00:08:29,915
By 2001,
a refined version of Gallager's codes

145
00:08:30,145 --> 00:08:33,615
came within four hundredths of a decibel
of Shannon's limit.

146
00:08:33,957 --> 00:08:38,017
That's less than one percent more signal
power than the theoretical minimum.

147
00:08:38,497 --> 00:08:41,537
The promise of 1948 had been kept.

148
00:08:44,117 --> 00:08:48,157
Today, Gallager's codes carry
much of the data in 5G phones,

149
00:08:48,237 --> 00:08:51,197
in modern Wi-Fi,
and in satellite television.

150
00:08:53,817 --> 00:08:55,637
Now, back to our eight bits.

151
00:08:56,317 --> 00:08:59,257
Three were lost, and there are eight ways
to fill the gaps.

152
00:08:59,838 --> 00:09:02,418
But these eight bits
were never just a message.

153
00:09:02,998 --> 00:09:05,578
They carried four bits of data,
Hamming's three checks,

154
00:09:05,808 --> 00:09:07,778
and one more check over all eight.

155
00:09:08,427 --> 00:09:12,187
Of all two hundred and fifty-six
possible patterns of eight bits,

156
00:09:12,387 --> 00:09:14,467
only sixteen follow those rules.

157
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:20,140
Test the eight candidates.
One by one, they fail. Only one is left.

158
00:09:20,823 --> 00:09:22,963
The missing bits were never really gone.

159
00:09:23,523 --> 00:09:26,523
Their information was still there,
in the bits that survived.

160
00:09:29,733 --> 00:09:31,613
So here's a question for you.

161
00:09:32,193 --> 00:09:36,173
Have you ever noticed error correction
at work in something you use every day?

162
00:09:36,473 --> 00:09:38,063
Let me know in the comments.
